---
/**
 * About: the public biography. Facts come only from copy the owner has already published (the
 * founder note on hllmr.com, 2026-08, and the essay); anything else waits for the owner's CV.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHead from "../../components/PageHead.astro";
import Entry from "../../components/Entry.astro";
import Pending from "../../components/Pending.astro";
import { HUB, LINKS, LOCALITY, NAME } from "../../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About · ${NAME}`}
  description="David Hellmer builds software the way he was trained to build control systems: the Navy's nuclear program, twenty years in building systems, and now AI-assisted development."
  ogType="profile"
>
  <PageHead k="About" title="About" lede="I build control systems, and lately I build software the way I was trained to build control systems." />

  <div class="log wrap">
    <Entry no="01" label="Portrait">
      <div class="split">
        <Pending what="Portrait photograph">
          <p>The owner will supply the photo. It ships with metadata (EXIF, GPS) stripped, in AVIF and JPEG, with alt text.</p>
        </Pending>
        <dl class="facts">
          <dt>Name</dt>
          <dd>{NAME}</dd>
          <dt>Based</dt>
          <dd>{LOCALITY}</dd>
          <dt>Work</dt>
          <dd>Founder, <a href={HUB}>HLLMR Ventures LLC</a></dd>
          <dt>Before</dt>
          <dd>U.S. Navy nuclear program; building automation and integrated building systems</dd>
          <dt>Writes at</dt>
          <dd><a href={LINKS.hubWriting}>hllmr.com/writing</a></dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </Entry>

    <Entry no="02" label="The short version" id="bio">
      <h2 id="bio">The short version</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I spent twenty years in building automation and integrated building systems: the controls, physical security,
          fire and networks that run a building, and the commissioning discipline that proves they do what the drawings say.
          Before that I was in the Navy's nuclear program, where you assume nothing works until you have watched it work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now I build software with AI coding agents every day, and the same discipline turns out to be the missing piece.
          An agent will report a task complete when it is not. A control nobody has watched deny anything is an assumption.
          A plan regenerated from the code launders drift into the record. None of that is new. It is the failure mode
          every commissioning engineer already knows, arriving in an industry that has not learned to distrust a status
          point yet.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Entry>

    <Entry no="03" label="Now" id="now">
      <h2 id="now">What I do now</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I run <a href={HUB}>HLLMR Ventures</a>, a small company in Texas where new ventures start: a software product,
          a development practice, and in time a music and events business. Each one has to prove itself, and when it does it
          gets its own name, look and address.
        </p>
        <p>
          The company runs on infrastructure it operates itself, with AI agents doing much of the work inside written roles
          and a person approving every change that ships.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="more" href="/ventures/">The ventures</a>
    </Entry>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .split :global(.pending) {
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    margin: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .split :global(.pending) {
      aspect-ratio: auto;
      min-height: 180px;
    }
  }
</style>
